The regex engine, in the order a pattern goes through it.

### Parsing

- `regex_parser.hpp`: Python's grammar, one pass over the UTF-8 pattern with a fixed stack of open
  groups (no recursion, no allocation), so the same code checks a literal in a consteval
  constructor and builds the tree at runtime. What it recognises goes to a sink.
- `regex_validator.hpp`: the sink that builds nothing, for `regex_pattern::check`.
- `regex_tree.hpp`: the sink that builds the tree (`regex_node.hpp`: nodes linked by index), with
  every character set resolved there to sorted code point ranges.
- `regex_ranges.hpp`: that set algebra: sorting and merging ranges, complements within the
  scalar values, the named classes (`\w \d \s`, Unicode from
  `2-vocabulary/unicode/impl/class_ranges.hpp` or ASCII), and the caseless closure of
  `ignore_case` from the caseless cycles of the same generated header.
- `regex_errors.hpp`: one undefined function per syntax error, called by the consteval check.

### Compiling

- `regex_compiler.hpp`: the tree to a Thompson NFA (`regex_program.hpp`: `regex_inst`, transitions
  of byte ranges), each node compiled knowing its successor. Counted repeats are unrolled; a set
  becomes a trie of the UTF-8 sequences of its ranges (`utf8_sequences.hpp` splits a range into
  byte range sequences), built as the sorted sequences stream in, identical states shared.
  A loop whose body can match empty gets a hidden slot holding where its iteration began and a
  `progress` instruction at the end of the body: leave when nothing was consumed, as Python's re.
- `regex_analysis.hpp`: what the matcher can skip by: the bytes a match can start with, whether
  it is anchored at the text's start, the literal every match starts with.

### Matching

`pike_vm.hpp` / `.cpp`: the Pike VM. Two thread lists (sparse sets: O(1) clear, insertion order is
priority), the epsilon closure walked with an explicit stack that also restores capture slots,
one byte of text per step. Every thread is told apart by its instruction and, inside a loop that
can iterate empty, by whether its iteration is still empty (`guards`): two threads differing in
that would end differently, so neither may stand for the other. All memory comes from one scratch
block the regex takes when made. `regex_names.hpp` looks group names up; `regex_replacement.hpp`
walks sub's templates, checking them before any text is produced.
